1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,500
A fault has cut power to 700 customers, including a community hospital.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,970
One wrong switch can energize the damaged cable.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:13,200
Restoration Lab turns high-stakes reasoning into a safe, repeatable drill.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:17,700
The one-line board is synthetic, local and built for junior distribution engineers.

5
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,700
Closing Source A too soon is blocked before the network changes.

6
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:32,200
The exact unsafe path flashes red.

7
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:35,200
Now the trainee opens both isolators.

8
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:38,000
Source A safely restores West campus.

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,900
Backup Source B picks up Riverside and the hospital.

10
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:47,650
Four accepted actions. 700 customers restored. The fault stays dead.

11
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,800
Every verdict comes from deterministic graph checks.

12
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:58,000
Fault reachability, dual-source backfeed, source capacity and critical-load restoration.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,200
No live grid data. No operational command. No black-box answer.

14
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:11,250
The repo keeps the plan, tests and code tour together, so the build can be inspected.

15
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:18,200
Restoration Lab is a focused proof of concept for learning safe restoration order.

16
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,480
See the consequence before a mistake happens.
